./README.md:9:Use Node.js 22.21 or newer. Install with `npm ci`, copy `.env.example` to `.env.local`, then set `GEMINI_API_KEY` and `GRADIUM_API_KEY`. Run `npm run dev` and open http://127.0.0.1:3000.
./README.md:11:Keys stay on the server. The page reports missing provider configuration; it does not substitute canned responses. Conversational turns default to `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`; feedback defaults to `gemini-3.8-flash`. Set `GEMINI_CONVERSATION_MODEL` and `GEMINI_FEEDBACK_MODEL` to override them independently. A legacy `GEMINI_MODEL` setting remains the fallback for either role when its specific setting is absent. The studio offers Marshall (male, default) and Lainey (female), with matching characters. `GRADIUM_VOICE_ID` remains the fallback for API calls that omit a voice choice. Google Cloud credits and Gemini API billing may differ: use credentials for the endpoint your event account provides.
./README.md:13:## Gradium hackathon setup
./README.md:15:The [hackathon guide](https://mesquite-pyrite-bca.notion.site/redeem-coupon) funds the voice integration already wired into this app: continuous microphone → Gradium streaming Speech-to-Text → Gemini reply → Gradium streamed Text-to-Speech → spoken counterpart. The coupon is redeemed in your Gradium account; it is not an application API or a source of conversation facts.
./README.md:17:1. Get the event's private redemption link from the organizer and redeem it in [Gradium Studio](https://studio.gradium.ai/), using the same email as your hackathon registration.
./README.md:18:2. Confirm the credited balance in [Billing](https://studio.gradium.ai/platform/billing), then create a key in [API Keys](https://studio.gradium.ai/platform/api-keys).
./README.md:19:3. Set `GRADIUM_API_KEY` in the project's ignored `.env.local` file and restart `npm run dev`. Keep the coupon and key out of the repository and browser code.
./README.md:20:4. With the app running at `http://127.0.0.1:3000`, run `npm run check:gradium`. If Next chooses a different port, set `APP_URL`, for example `APP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3001 npm run check:gradium`. This makes one short, credit-consuming synthesis call and transcribes that generated audio through our real app routes. It sends a fixed test sentence, not your conversations, and prints no credentials.
./README.md:22:For a distinctive counterpart, use [Voice Design](https://studio.gradium.ai/voices/design) to create and save a synthetic voice. Add its permanent `voice_id` to the server-approved choices in `src/lib/voices.ts` to expose it in the studio; `GRADIUM_VOICE_ID` sets the fallback for API calls without a selection. Example description: “A calm English-speaking hiring manager, clear diction, measured pace, warm but professional.”
./README.md:24:For the submission, identify the features as Gradium WebSocket STT and streamed REST TTS, include the selected voice ID, and explain the flow above. TTS currently omits `model_name`, so Gradium chooses its production default; see the [TTS guide](https://docs.gradium.ai/guides/text-to-speech-rest). Coupon redemption, available balance and actual provider access must be checked in your account. The voice check confirms the API path; also try a microphone turn to check recording and playback.
./README.md:30:Click **Start live voice** for hands-free conversation. The microphone stays open while the counterpart speaks; starting to speak interrupts pending responses and scheduled playback. Gradium semantic turn detection plus a short quiet period sends each answer automatically. A one-second quiet fallback handles missing turn signals. Acknowledged flush boundaries retain late transcript words. **Stop live voice**, reset, and finishing close the microphone and connection. The existing **Record your answer** button remains a push-to-talk fallback: click to record, then finish your turn. Audio is encoded as actual mono PCM WAV, not WebM relabelled as WAV. Audio goes to Gradium; the transcript goes to Gemini; Gemini's reply goes to Gradium for spoken output. A typed turn skips transcription. The avatar has idle, listening, thinking and speaking states.
./README.md:32:Spoken replies stream mono 48 kHz PCM16 into Web Audio playback as chunks arrive, rather than buffering a full WAV before speaking. The transcript still appears as soon as Gemini finishes. "Preparing voice" distinguishes synthesis from Gemini thinking. This reduces the audio download wait; the completed user turn and Gemini generation still precede synthesis. Duplex capture runs simultaneously with playback, but Gemini text generation and speech synthesis do not yet overlap.
./README.md:36:Live sessions use mono PCM in 80 ms frames, Gradium’s stable `default` STT model, and the supported minimum seven-frame transcription delay. The browser obtains a fresh single-use token through the server; the permanent key stays private. Token issuance is restricted to localhost for this account-free demo. Authenticate token issuance before public deployment. Capture requests echo cancellation, noise suppression and automatic gain control; use headphones for the first microphone test. Sessions stop after ten minutes, and individual turns are limited to 4,000 characters.
./README.md:43:| Microphone | `src/hooks/`, `src/lib/audio.ts`, `src/lib/duplex-session.ts` | Continuous PCM capture, turn detection, WAV fallback and cleanup |
./README.md:45:| Voice services | `src/app/api/transcribe/`, `src/app/api/speak/` | Gradium STT/TTS |
./README.md:63:Speech voice IDs are `marshall` and `lainey`, mapped to verified public Gradium catalogue IDs on the server. Speech format accepts `wav` or `pcm`. PCM streaming failures after response headers close the stream; the client retains the transcript and shows a speech error. Reset cancels the request and queued playback.
./README.md:92:Both public catalogue choices were verified with real Gradium requests. Through the app's PCM endpoint, the first bytes arrived in 81–226 ms, while each short test phrase finished downloading in 748–879 ms. Browser tests prove playback starts before stream EOF, the selected voice matches the name/avatar, and browser audio failure preserves the typed conversation. These are short synthetic checks, not a latency guarantee for every session.
./README.md:98:## Duplex verification — 10 October 2026
./README.md:100:Continuous capture, acknowledged turn boundaries, interruption during response generation and playback, two-turn history, permission cancellation, and stop/reset/feedback cleanup have focused coverage. All 108 unit/API tests and 16 browser tests pass. Instrumented service/audio coverage is 94.73% statements and 87.92% branches; lint, typecheck and the Webpack production build pass. A real short-lived token connected to Gradium STT at 24 kHz with seven-frame delay and acknowledged flushes. A synthetic spoken sentence streamed through that connection was correctly transcribed. Physical microphone echo cancellation and room acoustics still need a headset/speaker rehearsal.
